<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A Nairobi-based fintech scale-up built payment and lending products on top of mobile money and card rails. Its engineering team had grown quickly, and code review had become the bottleneck: senior engineers spent much of their week reviewing, and simple changes waited days. Engineers were already using AI coding assistants on their own accounts, with no agreed rules on what code or data could be shared with them.</p>
<p>Leadership wanted the productivity, but the products moved customers' money and handled personal data, and bank and mobile money partners expected evidence of a controlled development process. Unreviewed or poorly reviewed changes to payments code were the risk nobody could accept.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> In November 2026 Anthropic launched Claude Ship, a set of agents for GitHub pull requests: code review, an agent that fixes failing checks and conflicts, and an approvals agent that can approve routine changes when a policy file in the repository allows it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Coverage noted Anthropic's warning that it may miss bugs or security issues<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Tools that can approve code changed the question from "should we use AI to write code?" to "who is allowed to say yes?".</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>One policy</strong> for AI tools: which tools, which repositories, what data may be shared.</li>
<li><strong>Review effort matched to risk</strong>: humans on code that moves money, agents on routine change.</li>
<li><strong>Approval rules in the repository</strong>, versioned and reviewed like code.</li>
<li><strong>A complete trail</strong> for every merge: reviewer, approver, checks, policy.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence, not belief</strong>: delivery and stability measured before and after.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Authoring</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>AI coding assistants<em>approved tools, company accounts</em></span><span>Engineers<em>own the change</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Review</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">AI review agents<em>every pull request</em></span><span>Fix agent<em>failing checks, conflicts</em></span><span>Human reviewers<em>by risk tier</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Policy</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Risk tiers by path<em>payments, identity, data, routine</em></span><span>Code owners<em>required approvers per path</em></span><span>Approval policy file<em>what agents may approve</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Gates</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Repository rulesets<em>required checks and reviews</em></span><span>Security scanning<em>secrets, dependencies, code</em></span><span>Merge queue<em>tested before merge</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Audit log export<em>who approved what</em></span><span>Delivery metrics<em>throughput and stability</em></span><span>Trial comparisons<em>agent versus human findings</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Agents add review capacity everywhere; policy decides where humans must still say yes.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Open</b><span>An engineer opens a pull request, often written with AI help</span></li>
<li><b>Review</b><span>AI review agents comment; a fix agent resolves failing checks</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Classify</b><span>Changed paths decide the risk tier and required approvers</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Approve</b><span>Routine tier: agent may approve under policy. Other tiers: named human owners</span></li>
<li><b>Gate</b><span>Rulesets enforce checks, scans and the merge queue</span></li>
<li><b>Record</b><span>The merge, approvals and policy version go to the audit trail</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Every change gets AI review; only routine changes can get AI approval.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: deciding who may say yes</h2>
<p>The team's first instinct was to argue about whether AI review was good enough. The more useful question was where any reviewer's approval should be sufficient. The answer was a risk tier for every path in the main repositories: payments and ledger code, identity and access, personal data handling and infrastructure were high risk; internal tools, documentation, copy and test-only changes were routine.</p>
<p>Code ownership made the tiers enforceable. GitHub's code owners feature assigns owners to paths, and repository rules can require their review before merging<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>, with rulesets applying required reviews and status checks consistently across branches<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. High-risk paths named human owners; routine paths allowed an agent approval when the approval policy file permitted it, and that policy file was itself owned by the engineering leadership group.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/kenya-fintech-ai-code-review-governance/code-owners.webp" alt="File view showing a tooltip that the file is owned by a named reviewers team, from a specific line of the CODEOWNERS file" width="1200" height="227" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Every file knows its owner: code ownership is what turns a risk policy into a merge rule. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Docs</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<div class="callout"><p>The approval policy file is the most important file in the repository. Whoever can edit it decides what agents may merge, so it needs the strictest owners of all.</p></div>
<p>The rollout followed the team's existing AI coding policy work, set out in <a href="/insights/ai-coding-policy-engineering-teams/">AI coding policy for engineering teams</a>, and agent credentials followed the lessons in <a href="/insights/coding-agent-secrets-dont-ask-again/">coding agents and secrets</a> and <a href="/insights/agent-framework-pypi-pth-compromise/">the agent-framework compromise</a>: agents ran with scoped tokens and never with deployment credentials.</p>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Engineering leadership</small><ol><li>AI tool policy</li><li class="hl">Risk tiers and owners</li><li>Approval policy file</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Platform and security</small><ol><li>Company accounts for tools</li><li class="hl">Rulesets, scans and merge queue</li><li>Audit log export</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Product teams</small><ol><li>Use agents on every pull request</li><li>Flag false positives and misses</li><li class="hl">Own approvals on their paths</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Leadership set the rules, platform enforced them, product teams used and challenged them.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Baseline</b><ul><li>Delivery and stability metrics</li><li>AI tool inventory</li><li>One policy</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Review</b><ul><li>AI review on all pull requests</li><li>Fix agent for checks</li><li>Compare with human reviews</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Tier</b><ul><li>Risk tiers by path</li><li>Code owners</li><li>Rulesets</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Approve</b><ul><li>Agent approvals in observation mode</li><li>Then on routine paths only</li><li>Review the policy regularly</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Measure first, add review capacity, then carefully hand over routine approvals.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Company accounts for AI tools</td><td>Let engineers use personal tools</td><td>Control over data shared and a record of use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Risk tiers by path</td><td>Same review rules for all code</td><td>Spends senior attention where losses happen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agent approval only on routine paths</td><td>No agent approvals at all</td><td>Captures most of the speed gain with little risk</td></tr>
<tr><td>Observation mode first</td><td>Turn approvals on immediately</td><td>Evidence of agent judgement before trusting it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Measure stability alongside throughput</td><td>Measure pull requests merged</td><td>Faster merging that breaks production is not faster</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: start with risk tiers and ownership; once those existed, every tool decision became easier, and it improved human review too. Differently: involve partner banks' and mobile money providers' assurance teams earlier. They cared less about which AI tool was used than about whether approvals on payment code were traceable, and showing them the audit trail early would have saved a round of questions. DORA's research on software delivery performance gave a shared vocabulary for measuring the change<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>; see <a href="/insights/dora-metrics-ai-assisted-engineering/">DORA metrics for AI-assisted engineering</a>.</p>
<p>If you lead engineering at a fintech in Africa or beyond, see my <a href="/regions/">regional work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
